Yes. Once you separate those clocks, you get a much richer model than “time feels fast or slow.”

You can distinguish at least four rates:

R_{in}=\text{input / sampling rate}

R_{proc}=\text{processing / integration rate}

R_{sim}=\text{internal simulation rate}

R_{out}=\text{rendering / action rate}

The ratios between them matter more than the raw numbers.

If

R_{in} > R_{proc}

you accumulate backlog, compress, discard, or rely on heuristics.

If

R_{proc} > R_{out}

you may internally resolve many possibilities before producing one visible action.

If

R_{sim} \gg R_{world}

you can mentally run multiple candidate futures during a small amount of external time.

And if rendering is slower than processing, an observer may badly underestimate how much internal work occurred between two visible outputs.

Then make it networked. Different nodes can run at different temporal scales:

G=(V,E,\tau)

where each node has its own sampling, processing, memory, and response latency, and each edge has transmission delay and bandwidth.

Now the interesting deltas become things like:

\Delta_{perception}
=
t_{\text{event}}-t_{\text{noticed}}

\Delta_{decision}
=
t_{\text{noticed}}-t_{\text{decision}}

\Delta_{render}
=
t_{\text{decision}}-t_{\text{observable action}}

\Delta_{feedback}
=
t_{\text{action}}-t_{\text{consequence observed}}

That gives you a temporal topology of the system.

A market, company, nervous system, online community, battlefield simulation, or supply chain can all have fast local loops embedded inside slower global ones. One part can update every millisecond while another updates quarterly.

And mismatched clocks create characteristic phenomena:

* fast processor + slow feedback → overcorrection risk
* slow processor + fast environment → stale decisions
* fast rendering + shallow processing → reactive behavior
* slow rendering + deep simulation → apparently quiet system with large latent change
* delayed feedback loops → oscillation and instability
* multiple coupled clocks → phase locking, lag, resonance, aliasing

That last one is especially useful. Two systems can look unrelated until you measure their timing. Then you discover that one repeatedly responds three steps after another.

So your expanded operator becomes something like:

\text{state}
\rightarrow
\text{sample}
\rightarrow
\text{process}
\rightarrow
\text{simulate}
\rightarrow
\text{render}
\rightarrow
\text{feedback}

with a separate clock on every arrow.

Then Recursive Delta Observatory can operate not only on what changed, but on when each layer knew, processed, represented, acted, and learned the change.

That is closer to a chronodynamic map than a simple timeline: a web of heterogeneous clocks, latencies, bandwidths, and synchronization relationships.

And once you have that map, “speed” stops being one variable. It becomes a vector.